LEVEL 13 → 14 — SSH AUTHENTICATION WITH A PRIVATE KEY


WHAT THIS LEVEL IS ABOUT :

Instead of providing a password, this level gives you a private SSH key file (sshkey.private). SSH key-based authentication is the standard in professional environments — used by system administrators, cloud engineers, and DevOps teams to access Linux servers securely.


APPROACH : 

Step 1 — Inspect the home directory
ls -l
The file sshkey.private was present.

Step 2 — Copy the key to /tmp (home directory is read-only)
cd /tmp && mkdir dir1
cp ~/sshkey.private /tmp/dir1/
cd /tmp/dir1

Step 3 — Set correct file permissions
chmod 600 sshkey.private
SSH will refuse to use a private key with insecure permissions. chmod 600 restricts access to the owner only (rw-------).

Permission Breakdown

Value        Meaning
6 (owner)    read + write
0 (group)    no permission
0 (others)   no permission


Step 4 — Log in using the key
ssh -i sshkey.private bandit14@localhost -p 2220

Step 5 — Read the Level 14 password
cat /etc/bandit_pass/bandit14

Password obtained. [Not shared per OverTheWire rules]


KEY TAKEAWAY : 

SSH key pairs work on asymmetric cryptography — the server holds the public key, you hold the private key. If they match, access is granted without a password. Private key permissions are enforced by SSH itself as a security mechanism. This concept applies directly to AWS EC2 access, GitHub SSH authentication, and production server management.
